    "source": "process.stdout.write(\"# Find Bugs\\nReview code for functional bugs, logic errors, runtime crashes, race conditions, and regressions across a target scope (branch, PR, commit range, path, or folder).\\n---\\n## Target Scope & Resolution\\nAccept an optional target argument. If not specified, default to diffing the current branch against the repository's default branch.\\n### Resolution Logic:\\n* Default (No target specified):\\n  ```bash\\n  BASE_BRANCH=$(gh repo view --json defaultBranchRef --jq '.defaultBranchRef.name' 2>/dev/null || echo \\\"main\\\")\\n  git diff \\\"${BASE_BRANCH}...HEAD\\\"\\n  ```\\n* PR (e.g., `#123`, `123`, or GitHub PR URL):\\n  ```bash\\n  gh pr diff <PR_NUMBER_OR_URL>\\n  ```\\n* Branch or Commit Range (e.g., `staging`, `feature-auth`, `HEAD~3..HEAD`):\\n  ```bash\\n  git diff <TARGET>\\n  ```\\n* Directory or File Path within active diff (e.g., `src/services/billing/`, `lib/auth.ts`):\\n  ```bash\\n  BASE_BRANCH=$(gh repo view --json defaultBranchRef --jq '.defaultBranchRef.name' 2>/dev/null || echo \\\"main\\\")\\n  git diff \\\"${BASE_BRANCH}...HEAD\\\" -- <PATH>\\n  ```\\n* Directory or File Path (Static/Whole-file Audit, no diff):\\n  * If reviewing existing files directly (not a diff), locate and read all files within `<PATH>`.\\n---\\n## Phase 1: Input & Contract Gathering\\n1. Resolve target and fetch the full content/diff using the commands above.\\n2. If output is truncated, read each target file individually until every line in scope has been inspected.\\n3. List all files and entry points in scope.\\n4. Map every modified/target function, API handler, component, and data structure against call sites and upstream/downstream contracts.\\n---\\n## Phase 2: Execution Path & State Tracing\\nFor each file in scope, trace:\\n* Data Flow & Transformations: Inputs, intermediate states, and final returned/persisted values.\\n* Async & Lifecycle Spans: Promises, subscriptions, component lifecycles, and transaction boundaries.\\n* State Mutations: Where state is read, updated, cached, or synchronized across client/server boundaries.\\n* Branching & Fallbacks: Every conditional (`if`/`else`, `switch`, early return, catch block, default fallback).\\n---\\n## Phase 3: Defect Vector Checklist (Check EVERY in-scope path)\\n### 1. Logic & Control Flow\\n* Inverted / Broken Conditions: Flipped booleans, incorrect operator precedence (`||` vs `&&`), truthy/falsy coercion bugs (e.g., `0`, `\\\"\\\"`, or `false` failing truthy checks).\\n* Off-by-One & Indexing: Loop boundaries, slice/range bounds, pagination offsets, array index bounds.\\n* State Machine & Branch Invariants: Unreachable branches, missing cases in pattern matches/switches, early returns that bypass required side-effects or cleanups.\\n### 2. Async, Concurrency & Race Conditions\\n* Unhandled Async / Floating Promises: Missing `await`, unhandled promise rejections, fire-and-forget async operations dropping errors.\\n* State Races: Out-of-order responses overwriting newer data, simultaneous writes without locking/transactions, read-modify-write data loss.\\n* Cancellation & Cleanup: Missing abort signals on superseded requests, timers/listeners not cleaned up on unmount or task completion.\\n### 3. Null, Undefined & Boundary Contracts\\n* Unsafe Access: Missing null/undefined checks, unsafe optional chaining assumptions, unchecked map/array lookups.\\n* Type Assertions & Contract Mismatches: Unsafe type casts (`as unknown as T`, `any`, untyped JSON parsing) hiding runtime shape drift.\\n* Boundary Values: Behavior with empty arrays, empty strings, `0`, negative numbers, `NaN`, extreme payloads, or duplicate IDs.\\n### 4. Error Handling & Inconsistent State\\n* Swallowed Errors: Empty `catch` blocks or catches that log but leave state broken/hung in a loading state.\\n* Partial Failures & Incomplete Rollbacks: Multi-step operations failing midway through without resetting local state or rolling back DB transactions.\\n* Misleading Fallbacks: Fallback values masking real underlying failures.\\n### 5. Data Integrity, Cache & Sync\\n* Stale Reads / Cache Invalidation: Caches not updated after mutations, optimistic updates failing to roll back on server error.\\n* Reference vs Value Mutation: Accidental in-place mutation of props, frozen objects, or shared store references.\\n* Serialization / Parsing Drift: Date format/timezone mismatches, BigInt/number precision loss, snake_case vs camelCase key mismatches.\\n---\\n## Phase 4: Verification & False-Positive Elimination\\nFor each suspected issue:\\n1. Trace Callers & Guards: Verify if the condition is already guarded upstream or guaranteed impossible by the caller.\\n2. Inspect Existing Tests: Check if existing test suites assert this exact behavior or miss the edge case.\\n3. Confirm Reproducibility: Formulate a concrete input or sequence of events that triggers the failure.\\n---\\n## Phase 5: Pre-Conclusion Audit\\nBefore outputting findings, explicitly verify:\\n* [ ] Confirmed target scope and listed all inspected files.\\n* [ ] Every execution branch and error path was evaluated against the checklist.\\n* [ ] Unverified assumptions or areas needing broader integration context are clearly noted.\\n---\\n## Output Format\\n### Priority Order\\n1. Critical: Runtime crashes, data loss/corruption, stuck infinite loops, broken core workflows.\\n2. High: Incorrect business logic, race conditions under normal concurrency, silent failures/unhandled rejections.\\n3. Medium: Edge-case bugs (empty lists, boundaries), stale cache/UI states, missing cleanup/leaks.\\n4. Low: Minor logic discrepancies that do not cause crashes or data corruption.\\nSkip stylistic, formatting, or purely aesthetic suggestions.\\n### Issue Format\\nFor each verified bug:\\n* Location: `File:Line` — Short Title\\n* Severity: Critical | High | Medium | Low\\n* Target Scope: (e.g., `PR #42`, `branch vs main`, `src/billing/`)\\n* Bug Mechanism: What is broken and under what concrete condition/input it fails.\\n* Repro Scenario / Trace: Minimal sequence or input that exposes the defect.\\n* Impact: What the user, database, or system experiences when triggered.\\n* Suggested Fix: Concrete code example showing the fix.\\nIf no functional bugs are found, state that the target code is functionally sound—do not invent issues.\");"
